* Pointer receivers

You can declare methods with pointer receivers.

This means the receiver type has the literal syntax ⟪GTI18N_99f9fa4e_000001⟫*T⟪GTI18N_99f9fa4e_000002⟫ for some type ⟪GTI18N_99f9fa4e_000003⟫T⟪GTI18N_99f9fa4e_000004⟫.
(Also, ⟪GTI18N_99f9fa4e_000005⟫T⟪GTI18N_99f9fa4e_000006⟫ cannot itself be a pointer such as ⟪GTI18N_99f9fa4e_000007⟫*int⟪GTI18N_99f9fa4e_000008⟫.)

For example, the ⟪GTI18N_99f9fa4e_000009⟫Scale⟪GTI18N_99f9fa4e_000010⟫ method here is defined on ⟪GTI18N_99f9fa4e_000011⟫*Vertex⟪GTI18N_99f9fa4e_000012⟫.

Methods with pointer receivers can modify the value to which the receiver
points (as ⟪GTI18N_99f9fa4e_000013⟫Scale⟪GTI18N_99f9fa4e_000014⟫ does here).
Since methods often need to modify their receiver, pointer receivers are more
common than value receivers.

Try removing the ⟪GTI18N_99f9fa4e_000015⟫*⟪GTI18N_99f9fa4e_000016⟫ from the declaration of the ⟪GTI18N_99f9fa4e_000017⟫Scale⟪GTI18N_99f9fa4e_000018⟫ function on line 16
and observe how the program's behavior changes.

With a value receiver, the ⟪GTI18N_99f9fa4e_000019⟫Scale⟪GTI18N_99f9fa4e_000020⟫ method operates on a copy of the original
⟪GTI18N_99f9fa4e_000021⟫Vertex⟪GTI18N_99f9fa4e_000022⟫ value.
(This is the same behavior as for any other function argument.)
The ⟪GTI18N_99f9fa4e_000023⟫Scale⟪GTI18N_99f9fa4e_000024⟫ method must have a pointer receiver to change the ⟪GTI18N_99f9fa4e_000025⟫Vertex⟪GTI18N_99f9fa4e_000026⟫ value
declared in the ⟪GTI18N_99f9fa4e_000027⟫main⟪GTI18N_99f9fa4e_000028⟫ function.

